Use Testcontainers. Spring Boot can wire the container's connection details into your app, so you never write the URL.

## Preferred: `@ServiceConnection`

Add `spring-boot-testcontainers` as a test dependency. Then annotate the container field [S6]:

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	@ServiceConnection
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() { }
}
```

Spring Boot defines a `Neo4jConnectionDetails` bean from the container. Auto-configuration uses that bean, and it overrides any connection-related properties [S6].

The same works for JDBC databases. `JdbcConnectionDetails`, `FlywayConnectionDetails` and `LiquibaseConnectionDetails` match containers of type `JdbcDatabaseContainer`. `R2dbcConnectionDetails` matches containers such as `PostgreSQLContainer` and `MySQLContainer`. Mongo, Cassandra, Kafka, Redis-style services and others are also covered [S2].

## Container as a Spring bean

You can declare the container as a `@Bean` in a `@TestConfiguration` and `@Import` it into the test. The docs say this is often combined with service connection annotations [S4]. Spring manages the container's lifecycle, and it is stopped when the application context shuts down [S3].

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class MyTestConfiguration {
	@Bean
	MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer() {
		return new MongoDBContainer(DockerImageName.parse("mongo:5.0"));
	}
}
```

The docs' example doesn't show `@ServiceConnection` on this bean method. Add it there to get the automatic wiring [S4].

## Alternative: `@DynamicPropertySource`

This is more verbose but more flexible. Use it when no service connection exists for your container, or when you need to set arbitrary properties [S7]:

```java
@Container
static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

@DynamicPropertySource
static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
	registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
}
```

The property value is a supplier, so it is read after the container starts and picks up the randomly mapped port [S7].

**Module:** `@DataJpaTest` comes from the `spring-boot-data-jpa-test` module [S6]. The docs I searched don't give Maven or Gradle coordinates, so I can't show a dependency snippet.

**What it sets up** [S6]:
- It scans for `@Entity` classes and configures Spring Data JPA repositories.
- If an embedded database is on the classpath, it configures one.
- It sets `spring.jpa.show-sql` to `true`, so SQL queries are logged. You can turn this off with the annotation's `showSql` attribute.
- It doesn't scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. Use `@EnableConfigurationProperties` to include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans.
- Tests are transactional and roll back after each test.
- You can inject a `TestEntityManager`, which is a test-oriented alternative to the standard `EntityManager`.
- A `JdbcTemplate` is also available [S1].

The docs also point to an appendix that lists the exact auto-configurations enabled. That list wasn't in the passages I retrieved.

**Package names in Boot 4:**
```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jpa.test.autoconfigure.TestEntityManager;
```
[S1]

**Disabling the rollback behavior** [S6]:
```java
@DataJpaTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyNonTransactionalTests { }
```

**Testing against a real database instead of the embedded one** [S1]:
```java
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests { }
```

To get `TestEntityManager` in other Spring-based tests, add `@AutoConfigureTestEntityManager`. Make sure the test runs in a transaction, for example with `@Transactional` [S1].

Yes. Use `@DynamicPropertySource`. The docs describe it as a more verbose but more flexible alternative to service connections. A static method with this annotation adds dynamic property values to the Spring `Environment`. [S1]

```java
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class MyIntegrationTests {

	@Container
	static Neo4jContainer neo4j = new Neo4jContainer("neo4j:5");

	@Test
	void myTest() {
		// ...
	}

	@DynamicPropertySource
	static void neo4jProperties(DynamicPropertyRegistry registry) {
		registry.add("spring.neo4j.uri", neo4j::getBoltUrl);
	}

}
```

The property value is supplied as a `Supplier` (`neo4j::getBoltUrl`). The container's runtime-assigned values, such as the mapped URL, are therefore resolved when the property is read. With this setup, the application's Neo4j beans connect to the Testcontainers-managed container. [S1]

**Lifecycle caveat:** when Testcontainers annotations manage the containers, as `@Container` does above, Testcontainers controls their lifecycle. Spring Boot then gives no guarantee about shutdown order. A container can stop before the beans that depend on it are cleaned up, which can cause connection-loss exceptions. [S2, S3]

If the context stays cached for a while, the docs recommend one of two alternatives:
- Declare the containers as Spring beans. [S2, S3]
- Import the container declarations with `@ImportTestcontainers`. [S2, S6]

Here is how `@ImportTestcontainers` is used: [S6]

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
@ImportTestcontainers(MyContainers.class)
class MyTestConfiguration {
}
```

The search results also cover `TestPropertyValues`. It adds static `key=value` properties to an environment and doesn't read values from a container. [S5]

The passages I found mention service connections as an alternative but don't describe them, so I can't say more about that option.

Annotate a field in your test class with `@MockitoBean`. Spring Framework provides this annotation, and it defines a Mockito mock for a bean in the `ApplicationContext` [S1]. If you need a spy instead, use `@MockitoSpyBean` [S1].

The documentation's `@WebFluxTest` example does this:

```java
import org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean;
import static org.mockito.BDDMockito.given;

@WebFluxTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private WebTestClient webClient;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		this.webClient.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN).exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Honda Civic");
	}
}
```

The mock replaces the `UserVehicleService` bean in the context, and you stub it with the usual Mockito API. You don't need a `@Configuration` class [S5].

The Spring Boot documentation I searched only briefly covers `@MockitoBean` and `@MockitoSpyBean`. It points to the Spring Framework documentation for the details [S1]. That means I can't tell you from these passages how replacement behaves in edge cases, such as multiple beans of the same type.

Use `@MockitoSpyBean`. It's a Spring Framework annotation that defines a Mockito spy for a bean in the `ApplicationContext` [S1]. It wraps the real bean, so the real behavior still runs and you can verify calls on it.

The Spring Boot documentation I can search only mentions the annotation briefly and points to the Spring Framework docs for details [S1]. It has no spy-specific code example, so the one below is my own sketch and isn't taken from the docs. The `@MockitoBean` import path in the docs' examples is `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean` [S2]. `@MockitoSpyBean` should be in the same package, but the passages don't show that.

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @MockitoSpyBean
    private MyService myService;   // real bean, wrapped in a spy

    @Test
    void callsService() {
        // exercise code that uses myService...
        verify(myService).doSomething();
    }
}
```

For the full details, such as stubbing on a spy and how bean selection works, see the Spring Framework documentation on bean overrides. The documentation I can search doesn't cover those.

Use `@WebMvcTest`. It comes from the `spring-boot-webmvc-test` module. It sets up only the Spring MVC layer, not the full application context, and it auto-configures `MockMvc` [S2].

**What it scans:** only `@Controller`, `@ControllerAdvice`, `@JacksonComponent`, `Converter`, `Filter`, `HandlerInterceptor`, `WebMvcConfigurer` and similar web-layer beans. Regular `@Component` and `@ConfigurationProperties` beans aren't picked up [S2]. If you need a `@ConfigurationProperties` bean, add `@EnableConfigurationProperties`. For extra configuration classes, use `@Import` [S2].

**Collaborators:** the controller's dependencies aren't loaded, so mock them with `@MockitoBean`. `@WebMvcTest` is often limited to a single controller [S2].

**Assertions:** if AssertJ is available, `MockMvcTester` is auto-configured as well [S2].

```java
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest;

@WebMvcTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN))
			.hasStatusOk()
			.hasBodyTextEqualTo("Hello World".equals("") ? "" : "Honda Civic");
	}
}
```

I added a stray ternary in the last assertion by mistake. The documented assertion is simply:

```java
.hasBodyTextEqualTo("Honda Civic");
```

**Alternative:** if you want the full context with `@SpringBootTest`, add `@AutoConfigureMockMvc`. That loads the whole context in a mock web environment without starting a server [S1, S3, S4]. The docs recommend `@WebMvcTest` when you only want the web layer [S5].

Use Spring REST Docs with `@AutoConfigureRestDocs`. It configures the `MockMvc` bean to generate snippets, and you don't need the REST Docs JUnit extension [S2].

**Basic usage**

The docs' example uses `@WebMvcTest` with `MockMvcTester`. It calls `.apply(document(...))` to write snippets for that request [S3]:

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserController.class)
@AutoConfigureRestDocs
class MyUserDocumentationTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@Test
	void listUsers() {
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/users").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN)).hasStatusOk()
			.apply(document("list-users"));
	}
}
```

The static import is `org.springframework.restdocs.mockmvc.MockMvcRestDocumentation.document`. `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` comes from `org.springframework.boot.restdocs.test.autoconfigure` and lives in the `spring-boot-restdocs` module [S2, S3].

`MockMvcTester` and plain `MockMvc` share the same underlying instance, so any configuration you apply to one applies to both [S3].

**Output location and URIs**

By default, snippets go to `target/generated-snippets` with Maven or `build/generated-snippets` with Gradle. `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` can override that directory. It can also set the host, scheme, and port that appear in documented URIs [S2].

**Generating snippets for every call**

If you define a `RestDocumentationResultHandler` bean, the auto-configuration calls `alwaysDo` with it. Every `MockMvc` call then generates the default snippets automatically. The output directory can be parameterized, for example with `{method-name}` [S1]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyResultHandlerConfiguration {

	@Bean
	public RestDocumentationResultHandler restDocumentation() {
		return MockMvcRestDocumentation.document("{method-name}");
	}
}
```

**Further customization**

If the attributes of `@AutoConfigureRestDocs` aren't enough, register a `RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer` bean. This example switches the snippet format to Markdown [S3]:

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRestDocsConfiguration implements RestDocsMockMvcConfigurationCustomizer {

	@Override
	public void customize(MockMvcRestDocumentationConfigurer configurer) {
		configurer.snippets().withTemplateFormat(TemplateFormats.markdown());
	}
}
```

Use **`RestTestClient`**. The docs recommend it for tests that call a started server [S1].

Start the server on a random port with `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT)`. Then add `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` and autowire the client. It resolves relative URIs against the running server and has its own API for verifying responses [S1]:

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

The imports are:
- `org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureRestTestClient`
- `org.springframework.test.web.servlet.client.RestTestClient`

**AssertJ style:** wrap the response spec with `RestTestClientResponse.from(spec)`, then use AssertJ assertions [S4]:

```java
ResponseSpec spec = restClient.get().uri("/").exchange();
RestTestClientResponse response = RestTestClientResponse.from(spec);
assertThat(response).hasStatusOk().bodyText().isEqualTo("Hello World");
```

**Alternatives:**
- **`WebTestClient`:** it has a similar API. It needs `spring-webflux` on the classpath and `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` [S4].
- **`TestRestTemplate`:** use `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate`. It needs the `spring-boot-restclient` dependency. That dependency enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, declare the dependency on the main classpath, not only the test classpath [S5].

If you need the port, `@LocalServerPort` injects it [S1].

Set `webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT` on `@SpringBootTest`. Each test run then starts a full server on a randomly chosen available port [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureRestTestClient
class MyRandomPortRestTestClientTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest(@Autowired RestTestClient restClient) {
		restClient
			.get().uri("/")
			.exchange()
			.expectStatus().isOk()
			.expectBody(String.class).isEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

The imports are `org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest`, `SpringBootTest.WebEnvironment`, `org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureRestTestClient` and `org.springframework.test.web.servlet.client.RestTestClient` [S2].

- **Getting the port:** Inject the port the server actually uses with `@LocalServerPort` [S2].
- **Client choice:** `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` gives you a `RestTestClient` that resolves relative URIs against the running server [S2].
  - If `spring-webflux` is on the classpath, you can use `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` and autowire a `WebTestClient` instead [S3].
  - The `spring-boot-resttestclient` module also provides `TestRestTemplate` [S3].
- **Management port:** If your app uses a separate management port, `RANDOM_PORT` also starts the management server on its own random port [S1].
- **Sliced tests:** These may need `@AutoConfigureWebServer` to get an embedded web server [S3].
- **Default behavior:** Plain `@SpringBootTest` doesn't start a server. It uses a mock environment [S4]. A real server is the way to test lower-level container behavior, such as custom error pages, which MockMvc can't cover [S5].

The helper is `TestRestTemplate`. It's in the `spring-boot-resttestclient` module, and it also needs `spring-boot-restclient` [S1]. The docs I found don't say how it handles redirects.

**What it is**
- It's a convenience alternative to Spring's `RestTemplate` for integration tests [S1].
- Create one directly with `new TestRestTemplate()`. You can also inject one with `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT or DEFINED_PORT)` plus `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate` [S2].
- URLs with no host and port go to the embedded server [S2].
- You can customize the injected instance through a `RestTemplateBuilder` bean [S2].

```java
@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class MyTests {
    @Autowired TestRestTemplate template;

    @Test
    void t() {
        ResponseEntity<String> r = template.getForEntity("/example", String.class);
        assertThat(r.getStatusCode()).isEqualTo(HttpStatus.OK);
    }
}
```

**Security**
- You can get a vanilla template or one that sends Basic HTTP authentication with a username and password [S1].

**Errors**
- The template is "fault tolerant". It doesn't throw exceptions on 4xx and 5xx responses. You check the status code on the returned `ResponseEntity` instead [S1].

**Redirects**
- The docs say Apache HttpClient 5.1 or later is recommended but not mandatory. If it's on the classpath, `TestRestTemplate` configures the client appropriately [S1]. They don't say what that configuration does for redirects.

**Alternatives**
- `RestTestClient` is a fluent client with response-verification assertions. Use it with `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient`. It resolves relative links to the running server [S1, S5].
- `WebTestClient` is the equivalent for WebFlux [S1].

**Dependency caveat**
- Adding `spring-boot-restclient` enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. If your main code uses that builder, put the dependency on the main classpath, not only the test classpath [S1].

Use `@TestConfiguration`. It adds beans on top of your application's primary configuration. It doesn't replace that configuration.

**Nested class in the test**

A nested `@TestConfiguration` class is used in addition to your primary configuration. A nested regular `@Configuration` class would be used instead of it [S4].

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @TestConfiguration
    static class Extra {
        @Bean
        MyService testService() { return new FakeMyService(); }
    }

    @Test
    void exampleTest() { /* ... */ }
}
```

I wrote this example myself. The docs excerpts I found describe nested `@TestConfiguration` classes but don't include this code.

**Top-level class, imported where needed**

You can also put `@TestConfiguration` on a top-level class. That tells component scanning to skip it, so it isn't picked up by every test. Import it explicitly in the tests that need it [S3]:

```java
@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestsConfiguration.class)
class MyTests {
    @Test
    void exampleTest() { }
}
```

**Things to know**
- An imported `@TestConfiguration` is processed before an inner-class one. It is also processed before any configuration found through component scanning. This only matters if you rely on bean overriding [S3].
- If you use `@ComponentScan` directly instead of `@SpringBootApplication`, you must register the `TypeExcludeFilter` on it [S3].
- Slice tests such as `@WebMvcTest` don't scan regular `@Component` beans. Use `@Import` on the test to add extra configuration classes [S6].
- Spring caches application contexts between tests that share the same configuration. Each different set of test configurations can therefore create another context, which makes the test run slower [S4].

The annotation is **`@JdbcTest`**. It comes from the `spring-boot-jdbc-test` module and is meant for tests that only need a `DataSource` and don't use Spring Data JDBC. [S1]

- By default it configures an in-memory embedded database and a `JdbcTemplate`. [S1]
- It doesn't scan regular `@Component` or `@ConfigurationProperties` beans. To include `@ConfigurationProperties` beans, use `@EnableConfigurationProperties`. [S1]
- Tests are transactional and roll back after each test by default. [S1]
- To turn off the rollback behavior, add `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)`: [S1]

```java
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.JdbcTest;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Propagation;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@JdbcTest
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
class MyTransactionalTests {

}
```

- To run against a real database instead of the embedded one, add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase`. [S1]

Related slice annotations:
- `@DataJdbcTest` is the same idea but also configures Spring Data JDBC repositories. [S2]
- `@DataJpaTest` is the JPA slice. [S3]
- `@JooqTest` is the jOOQ slice, and it configures a `DSLContext`. [S4]

Use `@JsonTest`. It auto-configures only the JSON mapper, so you can test serialization and deserialization without loading the full application context [S3].

**What it configures**

`@JsonTest` sets up whichever supported mapper is on your classpath [S3]:
- Jackson `JsonMapper`, plus any `@JacksonComponent` beans and Jackson `JacksonModule`s.
- The deprecated Jackson 2 `ObjectMapper`, plus `@JsonComponent` beans and Jackson `Module`s.
- `Gson`.
- `Jsonb`.

**Tester helpers**

The helpers are AssertJ-based and use JSONAssert and JsonPath. The classes are `JacksonTester`, `GsonTester`, `JsonbTester`, and `BasicJsonTester`. `BasicJsonTester` works on Strings. With `@JsonTest` you can `@Autowired` these helpers as fields [S3].

```java
@JsonTest
class MyJsonTests {

	@Autowired
	private JacksonTester<VehicleDetails> json;

	@Test
	void serialize() throws Exception {
		VehicleDetails details = new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic");
		// Assert against a `.json` file in the same package as the test
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).isEqualToJson("expected.json");
		// Or use JSON path based assertions
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).hasJsonPathStringValue("@.make");
		assertThat(this.json.write(details)).extractingJsonPathStringValue("@.make").isEqualTo("Honda");
	}

	@Test
	void deserialize() throws Exception {
		String content = "{\"make\":\"Ford\",\"model\":\"Focus\"}";
		assertThat(this.json.parse(content)).isEqualTo(new VehicleDetails("Ford", "Focus"));
		assertThat(this.json.parseObject(content).getMake()).isEqualTo("Ford");
	}

}
```
[S2]

**Notes**
- To customize the auto-configuration, use `@AutoConfigureJsonTesters` [S3].
- You can also use the helpers in plain unit tests without `@JsonTest`. In that case, call the helper's `initFields` method in a `@BeforeEach` method [S2].
- Number assertions at a JSON path may not work with `isEqualTo`, depending on the type. Use AssertJ's `satisfies` instead [S2]:

```java
assertThat(this.json.write(value)).extractingJsonPathNumberValue("@.test.numberValue")
	.satisfies((number) -> assertThat(number.floatValue()).isCloseTo(0.15f, within(0.01f)));
```
[S1]

Add `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)` to the test class. This stops Spring Boot from swapping your configured `DataSource` for an embedded database [S6]:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;

@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests {

	// ...

}
```

- By default, `@DataJpaTest` configures an embedded database if one is on the classpath [S8]. The annotation above turns that off.
- The same annotation works the same way for `@JdbcTest`, `@DataJdbcTest` and `@DataR2dbcTest` [S3, S5, S7].
- The documentation passages I found don't say how to provide the real database, for example through Testcontainers or `spring.datasource.*` properties. They only cover disabling the replacement.
- `@DataJpaTest` tests are still transactional and roll back after each test by default [S8]. You can change that with `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)` [S8].

Use Spring Boot's `OutputCaptureExtension`. It's a JUnit extension that captures `System.out` and `System.err` [S1].

Add `@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)` to the test class. Then inject `CapturedOutput` as a test method argument or a constructor argument [S1].

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.extension.ExtendWith;

import org.springframework.boot.test.system.CapturedOutput;
import org.springframework.boot.test.system.OutputCaptureExtension;

import static org.assertj.core.api.Assertions.assertThat;

@ExtendWith(OutputCaptureExtension.class)
class MyOutputCaptureTests {

	@Test
	void testName(CapturedOutput output) {
		System.out.println("Hello World!");
		assertThat(output).contains("World");
	}

}
```

The example asserts on the captured output with AssertJ's `contains`. The documentation I found doesn't describe other `CapturedOutput` methods.

Yes. Declare each container as a `@Bean` in a `@TestConfiguration` class, then import that class into your tests [S2].

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
class MyTestConfiguration {

    @Bean
    MongoDBContainer mongoDbContainer() {
        return new MongoDBContainer(DockerImageName.parse("mongo:5.0"));
    }
}

@SpringBootTest
@Import(MyTestConfiguration.class)
class MyIntegrationTests {

    @Autowired
    private MongoDBContainer mongo;
    ...
}
```

The docs say this approach is often combined with service connection annotations [S2]. Annotate the bean method with `@ServiceConnection`, and Spring Boot works out the connection details from the bean's return type. This works for typed containers such as `Neo4jContainer` or `RabbitMQContainer` [S6]. Spring Boot doesn't call the bean method to read the image name, because that would cause eager initialization problems [S6].

For a `GenericContainer`, Spring Boot can't tell which image is used. In that case, give it a hint with `name`:

```java
@Bean
@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
public GenericContainer<?> redisContainer() {
    return new GenericContainer<>("redis:7");
}
```

**Lifecycle benefits**
- Container beans are created and started before all other beans. They are stopped after all other beans are destroyed [S3]. Beans that use the container can therefore clean up while it's still available.
- A container bean is started once per application context managed by the TestContext Framework [S1]. It's stopped when that context shuts down, which usually happens after all tests using the cached context have finished [S1].
- One container instance is often reused across tests from multiple test classes [S1].

By contrast, containers managed by Testcontainers itself (static fields with its annotations and extensions) give no guarantee about shutdown order relative to beans. A container can stop before the beans that depend on it are cleaned up, and those beans can then throw exceptions such as connection loss [S1].

The docs also mention `@ImportTestcontainers` as another way to import container declarations [S1]. The passages I found don't describe how it works.

Annotate the container with `@ServiceConnection`. Spring Boot then creates a `DataRedisConnectionDetails` bean for it, and `@DataRedisTest` uses that bean to connect.

**1. Dependency.** Add `spring-boot-testcontainers` as a test dependency [S5]. For `@DataRedisTest` itself you need the `spring-boot-data-redis-test` module [S2]. I didn't find the Testcontainers/JUnit artifact coordinates in the passages, so I haven't listed them.

**2. Test.** I adapted this from the docs' Neo4j example [S5]. I haven't checked it against a Redis-specific example. It uses a `GenericContainer` with `name = "redis"`, which the docs support [S6].

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.testcontainers.containers.GenericContainer;
import org.testcontainers.junit.jupiter.Container;
import org.testcontainers.junit.jupiter.Testcontainers;

import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.data.redis.test.autoconfigure.DataRedisTest;
import org.springframework.boot.testcontainers.service.connection.ServiceConnection;

@Testcontainers
@DataRedisTest
class MyDataRedisTests {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
    static GenericContainer<?> redis = new GenericContainer<>("redis:7").withExposedPorts(6379);

    @Autowired
    private SomeRepository repository;

    // ...
}
