By default, `@SpringBootTest` doesn't call your `main` method. It builds the `ApplicationContext` directly from the configuration class, so anything `main` customizes, such as additional profiles, is skipped [S2].

To make the test go through `main`, set `useMainMethod` on the annotation [S1]:

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest.UseMainMethod;

@SpringBootTest(useMainMethod = UseMainMethod.ALWAYS)
class MyApplicationTests {

	@Test
	void exampleTest() {
		// ...
	}

}
```

With this setting, the profiles that `main` sets are active when the context starts. Your `main` might look like this [S1, S2]:

```java
public static void main(String[] args) {
	SpringApplication application = new SpringApplication(MyApplication.class);
	application.setBannerMode(Banner.Mode.OFF);
	application.setAdditionalProfiles("myprofile");
	application.run(args);
}
```

`useMainMethod` accepts these values [S1]:
- `ALWAYS`: the test fails if no `main` method can be found.
- `WHEN_AVAILABLE`: the test uses `main` if it exists. Otherwise it falls back to the standard loading mechanism.
- The default is to not use `main`.

