Spring Boot matches a container to connection details by the repository part of its Docker image name. That part ignores the registry and the version [S2]. Your image's repository is `mirror/myredis`, which doesn't match any name Spring Boot recognizes. For Redis, the recognized names are "redis", "redis/redis-stack" and "redis/redis-stack-server". Typed containers `RedisContainer` and `RedisStackContainer` also match [S2].

The `name` attribute overrides that lookup. The docs use your exact image as the example. With `registry.mycompany.com/mirror/myredis`, you use `@ServiceConnection(name="redis")` to make sure `DataRedisConnectionDetails` are created [S1].

```java
@TestConfiguration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
public class MyRedisConfiguration {

	@Bean
	@ServiceConnection(name = "redis")
	public GenericContainer<?> redisContainer() {
		return new GenericContainer<>("registry.mycompany.com/mirror/myredis:7");
	}

}
```

The same `name = "redis"` attribute works on a `static` container field.

If you declare the container as a `@Bean` method, Spring Boot doesn't call the method to get the image name. That would cause eager-initialization problems, so it uses the method's return type instead. A `GenericContainer` return type tells it nothing about the image, so `name` is required in that case [S1].

No. `@PemKeyStore` and `@PemTrustStore` only configure SSL on the client side, which is your application. The docs say you still have to enable SSL on the service inside the Testcontainer yourself [S1].

In the documented Redis example, the container is a custom `SecureRedisContainer`, a subclass of `RedisContainer`. It copies the certificates to the correct places and starts `redis-server` with command-line parameters that enable SSL [S1].

```java
@Container
@ServiceConnection
@PemKeyStore(certificate = "classpath:client.crt", privateKey = "classpath:client.key")
@PemTrustStore("classpath:ca.crt")
static RedisContainer redis = new SecureRedisContainer("redis:latest");
```

The annotations do two things on the client side [S1]:
- `@PemKeyStore` loads the client certificate and key. This lets the client authenticate against the server.
- `@PemTrustStore` loads the CA certificate. This makes sure the server certificate is valid and trusted.

Redis is among the services that support these annotations [S2]. Only `ElasticsearchContainer` gets automatic detection of server-side SSL, and that works through `@Ssl` [S2].

