import org.testcontainers.postgresql.PostgreSQLContainer;

import org.springframework.boot.data.jpa.test.autoconfigure.DataJpaTest;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase;
import org.springframework.boot.jdbc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestDatabase.Replace;
import org.springframework.boot.testcontainers.service.connection.ServiceConnection;

@Testcontainers
@DataJpaTest
@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)
class MyRepositoryTests {

    @Container
    @ServiceConnection
    static PostgreSQLContainer postgres = new PostgreSQLContainer("postgres:16");

    // @Autowired repositories / TestEntityManager, @Test methods...
}
```

1. **Disable the embedded database.** `@DataJpaTest` configures an embedded database if one is on the classpath. `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)` stops that and lets the test use a real database [S5].
2. **Declare the container.** The `@Testcontainers` and `@Container` annotations start the PostgreSQL container before the tests run [S8, S9].
3. **Connect it with `@ServiceConnection`.** Spring Boot creates connection details from the annotated container. These take precedence over connection-related properties, so you don't need to set `spring.datasource.*` [S9]. A `PostgreSQLContainer` produces `JdbcConnectionDetails` and `R2dbcConnectionDetails` [S11].

You need `spring-boot-testcontainers` as a test dependency to use service connections [S9]. Keep the container in a `static` field so Spring Boot can read the container instance [S11].

The docs I found show the `@ServiceConnection` example with a Neo4j container, not PostgreSQL. I adapted it to PostgreSQL using the service-connection table, which lists `JdbcConnectionDetails` for `JdbcDatabaseContainer` types [S7]. The `PostgreSQLContainer` import package and the `postgres:16` image tag come from my own assumptions, not from the docs, so check them against your Testcontainers version.

Use two annotations: `@AutoConfigureHttpGraphQlTester` creates the `HttpGraphQlTester` bean, and `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` binds it to MockMvc [S1, S6].

**Getting the tester**

Annotate the test class with `@AutoConfigureHttpGraphQlTester`. It comes from the `spring-boot-graphql-test` module. Then autowire `HttpGraphQlTester` [S6].

**Choosing the transport**

The tester uses the transport of the integration test [S1]:
- With `RANDOM_PORT` or `DEFINED_PORT`, it is configured against the live server [S1].
- To bind it to MockMvc, add `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` to the test class [S1].
- `MOCK` is the default `webEnvironment`. It starts no embedded server and works with `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` [S4].

So the MockMvc setup is:

```java
import org.springframework.boot.graphql.test.autoconfigure.tester.AutoConfigureHttpGraphQlTester;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureMockMvc;
import org.springframework.graphql.test.tester.HttpGraphQlTester;

@SpringBootTest   // default WebEnvironment.MOCK, no server started
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
@AutoConfigureHttpGraphQlTester
class GraphQlMockMvcTests {

	@Test
	void greet(@Autowired HttpGraphQlTester graphQlTester) {
		graphQlTester.document("{ greeting(name: \"Alice\") } ")
			.execute()
			.path("greeting")
			.entity(String.class)
			.isEqualTo("Hello, Alice!");
	}
}
```

I wrote this example by combining the documented pieces. The docs only show the live-server version, which uses `webEnvironment = RANDOM_PORT` and `@AutoConfigureHttpGraphQlTester` [S6].

To customize the tester, for example to add auth headers, use `graphQlTester.mutate().webTestClient(...).build()`, as in the docs' `RANDOM_PORT` example [S6]. The docs don't say whether that customizer works the same way when the tester is bound to MockMvc.

**The cause:** your service uses `RestClient.Builder`, so the expectation has to use the full URI. The relative path `/greet/details` only works when the base URI was set through `RestTemplateBuilder.baseUri(...)`. The docs say [S1]:

> When using a `RestClient.Builder` in the beans under test, or when using a `RestTemplateBuilder` without calling `baseUri(String baseUri)`, the full URI must be used in the `MockRestServiceServer` expectations.

**The fix:** include the base URL in the expectation.

```java
@RestClientTest(RemoteVehicleDetailsService.class)
class MyRestClientServiceTests {

	@Autowired
	private RemoteVehicleDetailsService service;

	@Autowired
	private MockRestServiceServer server;

	@Test
	void getVehicleDetailsWhenResultIsSuccessShouldReturnDetails() {
		this.server.expect(requestTo("https://example.com/greet/details"))
			.andRespond(withSuccess("hello", MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN));
		String greeting = this.service.callRestService();
		assertThat(greeting).isEqualTo("hello");
	}
}
```

Replace `https://example.com` with whatever base URL your service configures [S1].

The relative-path form (`requestTo("/greet/details")`) is documented only for the `RestTemplateBuilder.baseUri(...)` case [S4].

