Spring Boot backs off from creating `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` in your situation. The documentation says:

> The `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` reuses the same `JdbcTemplate` instance behind the scenes. If more than one `JdbcTemplate` is defined and no primary candidate exists, the `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` is not auto-configured. [S2]

With two `JdbcTemplate` beans, Boot can't tell which one to wrap, so it creates no `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` bean. Injection then fails.

**Fix:** mark one `JdbcTemplate` as `@Primary`. Boot then wraps that one. The docs state the primary-candidate condition but don't show this snippet, so the code below is my own illustration.

```java
@Bean
@Primary
JdbcTemplate primaryJdbcTemplate(DataSource ds) {
    return new JdbcTemplate(ds);
}

@Bean
JdbcTemplate otherJdbcTemplate(@Qualifier("otherDataSource") DataSource ds) {
    return new JdbcTemplate(ds);
}
```

If you need a `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` for the non-primary database, define it yourself. For example, declare `new NamedParameterJdbcTemplate(otherJdbcTemplate)` as a `@Bean`. I'm inferring this from the docs and haven't seen it documented.

`JdbcClient` is auto-configured based on the presence of a `NamedParameterJdbcTemplate` [S1]. If that bean is missing, `JdbcClient` is probably affected too.

