// Boot 4
import org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest;
```

The docs' examples import it from the new package [S1].

Add `spring-boot-webmvc-test` as a test dependency. `spring-boot-test-autoconfigure` on its own no longer contains it. The passages I found don't say which starter pulls it in, so check your build.

Other MVC test classes moved in the same way. For example, `@AutoConfigureMockMvc` is now `org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureMockMvc` [S2]. Other test support also has new packages. `@AutoConfigureWebTestClient` is in `org.springframework.boot.webtestclient.autoconfigure`, and `@AutoConfigureRestTestClient` is in `org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure` [S2].

**Dependencies**

You need two modules:
- `spring-boot-resttestclient` provides `TestRestTemplate`. [S1]
- `spring-boot-restclient` is also required. [S1]

Apache HttpClient 5.1 or later is recommended but optional. If it's on the classpath, `TestRestTemplate` configures it appropriately. [S1]

The docs excerpts I found don't give Maven or Gradle coordinates, so I haven't written out a build snippet. The module names above are as the docs give them.

**Side effect on production code**

Adding `spring-boot-restclient` enables auto-configuration for `RestClient.Builder`. [S1, S3]

If your main code uses `RestClient.Builder`, declare `spring-boot-restclient` on the main classpath, not just the test classpath. [S1, S3] Otherwise the builder is auto-configured in your tests but not in production.

**Usage in a test**

In a `@SpringBootTest` with `RANDOM_PORT` or `DEFINED_PORT`, you must also annotate the class with `@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate` to inject the template. [S2]

```java
import org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.TestRestTemplate;
import org.springframework.boot.resttestclient.autoconfigure.AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate;

@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = WebEnvironment.RANDOM_PORT)
@AutoConfigureTestRestTemplate
class MyTests {
    @Test
    void exampleTest(@Autowired TestRestTemplate restTemplate) {
        String body = restTemplate.getForObject("/", String.class);
    }
}
```

In Spring Boot 4, you don't use `@MockBean`. The documentation I searched doesn't mention it. It describes Spring Framework's `@MockitoBean` for this job, and `@MockitoSpyBean` for spies [S1]. Both annotations define a Mockito mock or spy for a bean inside your `ApplicationContext` [S1].

The Spring Boot docs only briefly introduce these annotations and point to the Spring Framework documentation for details [S1]. They include no code example, so the sketch below is from my own knowledge and not from the docs. It assumes `MyService` is a bean in your context, `MyController` depends on it, and Mockito is on the classpath:

```java
@SpringBootTest
class MyTests {

    @MockitoBean
    MyService service;   // replaces the MyService bean in the context

    @Test
    void test() {
        when(service.greet()).thenReturn("hi");
        // ...
    }
}
```

