`@JsonTest` auto-configures whichever supported JSON mapper is available. The docs list four [S1]:

- **Jackson `JsonMapper`**, plus any `@JacksonComponent` beans and any Jackson `JacksonModule`.
- **Jackson 2 `ObjectMapper`**, plus any `@JsonComponent` beans and any Jackson `Module`. The docs mark this as deprecated.
- **`Gson`**.
- **`Jsonb`**.

So Jackson 2's `ObjectMapper` is still supported in `@JsonTest`, but it is deprecated. The docs steer you toward the new `JsonMapper` and `@JacksonComponent`. Elsewhere, `@JsonComponent` is also marked deprecated [S6].

The matching test helpers are `JacksonTester`, `GsonTester`, `JsonbTester` and `BasicJsonTester` (for Strings). You can `@Autowired` them in a `@JsonTest` class [S1].

```java
@JsonTest
class MyJsonTests {
    @Autowired
    private JacksonTester<VehicleDetails> json;

    @Test
    void serialize() throws Exception {
        assertThat(this.json.write(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic")))
            .extractingJsonPathStringValue("@.make").isEqualTo("Honda");
    }
}
```

To customize the setup, use `@AutoConfigureJsonTesters` [S1].

The passages I found don't say how the choice is made when more than one library is on the classpath. They also don't give a removal timeline for Jackson 2 support.

**Mocking a collaborator**

Put `@MockitoBean` on a field in the test class. `@WebMvcTest` is usually limited to a single controller and combined with `@MockitoBean` to provide mocks for the collaborators that controller needs [S4]. The mock replaces that bean in the test's `ApplicationContext` [S10]. Spring Framework also provides `@MockitoSpyBean` if you want a spy [S10].

```java
@WebMvcTest(UserVehicleController.class)
class MyControllerTests {

	@Autowired
	private MockMvcTester mvc;

	@MockitoBean
	private UserVehicleService userVehicleService;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		given(this.userVehicleService.getVehicleDetails("sboot"))
			.willReturn(new VehicleDetails("Honda", "Civic"));
		assertThat(this.mvc.get().uri("/sboot/vehicle").accept(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN))
			.hasStatusOk()
			.hasBodyTextEqualTo("Hello World");
	}
}
```

This is adapted from the documented example. The original expects the body `"Honda Civic"` [S2]. The imports are `org.springframework.test.context.bean.override.mockito.MockitoBean` and `org.springframework.boot.webmvc.test.autoconfigure.WebMvcTest` [S2].

