The passages do cover these related points:

- `@DataJpaTest` tests are transactional and roll back at the end of each test by default. [S4] Anything written during a test method is therefore undone afterwards.
- You can turn that off for a test or a whole class with `@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)`. [S4]
- If you only need a few rows, the documented seeding approach is `TestEntityManager`. You persist entities directly in the test, and then query through the repository. [S3, S4]

```java
@DataJpaTest
class MyRepositoryTests {

	@Autowired
	private TestEntityManager entityManager;

	@Autowired
	private UserRepository repository;

	@Test
	void testExample() {
		this.entityManager.persist(new User("sboot", "1234"));
		User user = this.repository.findByUsername("sboot");
		assertThat(user.getUsername()).isEqualTo("sboot");
	}
}
```

- A `JdbcTemplate` is also available in `@DataJpaTest` if you want to run SQL directly. [S3]
- To run against a real database instead of the embedded one, use `@AutoConfigureTestDatabase(replace = Replace.NONE)`. [S3]

For `@Sql` itself, see the Spring Framework testing documentation. It covers script locations, execution phase and transaction interaction. The Spring Boot reference I can search points there for transaction details. [S4]
